        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th class="sortable" data-column="0">Name</th>
                <th class="sortable" data-column="1">Age</th>
                <th class="sortable" data-column="2">City</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>John</td>
                <td>25</td>
                <td>New York</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Jane</td>
                <td>30</td>
                <td>Los Angeles</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Bob</td>
                <td>22</td>
                <td>Chicago</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <script>
        document.querySelectorAll('.sortable').forEach(header => {
            header.addEventListener('click', function() {
                const column = this.getAttribute('data-column');
                const tbody = document.querySelector('tbody');
                const rows = Array.from(tbody.querySelectorAll('tr'));
                const sortAsc = this.classList.contains('asc');
                const sortDesc = this.classList.contains('desc');

                rows.sort((a, b) => {
                    const aValue = a.cells[column].textContent;
                    const bValue = b.cells[column].textContent;

                    if (sortAsc) {
                        return aValue.localeCompare(bValue);
                    } else if (sortDesc) {
                        return bValue.localeCompare(aValue);
                    }
                    return 0;
                });

                rows.forEach(row => tbody.appendChild(row));

                // Toggle sort direction
                this.classList.remove('asc', 'desc');
                if (sortAsc) {
                    this.classList.add('desc');
                } else if (sortDesc) {
                    this.classList.add('asc');
                } else {
                    this.classList.add('asc');
                }
            });
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code sets up a basic sortable table where clicking on a header sorts the rows based on the column data. The `localeCompare` method is used for alphanumeric sorting.

What is the purpose of the `localeCompare` method in the sorting function?

The `localeCompare` method is used to compare two strings in a locale-aware manner. It returns a number indicating whether the first string comes before, after, or is the same as the second string. This method is particularly useful for sorting strings that contain numbers or special characters, as it handles these cases more accurately than simple string comparison. For example, "10" will be correctly sorted after "2" instead of before it, which would happen with a simple string comparison.

